    "id": "polymarket_0x2f676f8d85d60dd20ce0e0ba14608825b970a0491b419d7dedfa81bcf646af2b",
    "title": "Ukraine agrees to US-approved peace plan by Thursday?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "No background available.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This market will resolve to “Yes” if the Government of Ukraine publicly agrees to a U.S.-approved peace framework for the Russo–Ukrainian war, by November 27, 2025, 11:59 PM ET. Otherwise, this market will resolve to “No”.\n\nTo qualify, the agreement must be explicitly described as Ukraine’s formal acceptance of either (1) the U.S. 28-point peace plan, or (2) a revised or alternative peace framework that the United States publicly states it has approved. \n\nIf such an agreement is officially reached before the resolution date, this market will resolve to “Yes”, regardless of if/when the agreement is implemented, enters into force, or is fully carried out.\n\nCounter proposals by Ukraine, agreements to “treat the U.S. framework as a basis”, “work within the framework” or other ambiguous statements will not qualify unless they are clearly described by both sides or a consensus of credible reporting as Ukraine’s formal acceptance of a U.S.-approved peace framework. \n\nThe primary resolution source for this market will be a consensus of credible reporting.",
    "outcome": "No",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nUkraine did not agree to a US-approved peace plan by the Thursday, November 27/28, 2025 deadline. The diplomatic initiative, centered around a 20-point proposal crafted by US envoys Keith Kellogg and Steven Witkoff, collapsed after its controversial terms were leaked to the public. Facing intense opposition from Ukraine, European allies, and Russia, the negotiations failed, culminating in Kellogg's departure from his post and the definitive end of the plan.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2025-11-21 to 2025-11-23**: US envoys Keith Kellogg and Steven Witkoff push a 20-point peace plan to end the Russia-Ukraine war. [art_general_20251121_004_7f6a8465]\n- **2025-11-24**: Market sentiment and diplomatic optimism briefly peak regarding a potential agreement.\n- **2025-11-25**: The details of the Kellogg-Witkoff plan are leaked to the public, causing immediate diplomatic backlash.\n- **2025-11-26**: European allies and the Ukrainian delegation strongly reject the proposed terms, which include deferring NATO membership and setting restrictive US conditions on EU accession. [art_general_20251126_003_89adc863]\n- **2025-11-27**: Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov dismisses the framework, while US envoy Keith Kellogg announces his departure, marking the collapse of the peace push.\n- **2025-11-28**: The deadline passes with no agreement reached.\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe failure of the peace plan was rooted in the **controversial nature of the proposed terms** (deferring NATO membership, territorial concessions), which caused deep reservations among Ukrainian and European leaders. This skepticism was triggered into open opposition when the **plan was leaked to the public**, leading to public outcry and diplomatic embarrassment. The leak resulted in a unified rejection by European allies and Ukraine, who refused to accept the US-imposed conditions. Concurrently, Russia rejected the framework, removing any remaining viability. Ultimately, this insurmountable opposition led to the **resignation of envoy Keith Kellogg**, ending the diplomatic push and resulting in the final outcome where no agreement was reached by the deadline. [art_general_20251126_003_89adc863]\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe primary force pushing toward an agreement was the immense pressure from the incoming Trump administration to secure a swift diplomatic victory and end the war. This pressure created temporary optimism around November 24. However, this momentum was overwhelmed by the fundamental misalignment of the plan's terms with the core security requirements of Ukraine and Europe, as well as Russia's maximalist demands. \n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1**: US envoys present the 20-point peace plan.\n  - title: Presentation of peace plan\n  - Date: 2025-11-21\n  - why it matters causally: Initiated the diplomatic push and established the controversial terms.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20251121_004_7f6a8465]\n- **E2**: Leak of the peace plan details.\n  - title: Leak of peace plan\n  - Date: 2025-11-25\n  - why it matters causally: Triggered public backlash and forced stakeholders to publicly reject the terms.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20251126_003_89adc863]\n- **E3**: Keith Kellogg departs as US envoy.\n  - title: Collapse of the plan\n  - Date: 2025-11-27\n  - why it matters causally: Signaled the definitive collapse of the US peace initiative.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20251126_003_89adc863]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Presentation of peace plan | [art_general_20251121_004_7f6a8465] | 2025-11-21 | 0.9 | Plan associated with Trump envoys |\n| European/Ukrainian pushback | [art_general_20251126_003_89adc863] | 2025-11-26 | 0.9 | Leaks and rejection documented |\n| Collapse of the plan | [art_general_20251126_003_89adc863] | 2025-11-27 | 0.9 | Kellogg's departure seals failure |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nIt remains uncertain whether a different, more consultative diplomatic approach by the US could have secured partial agreement. An alternative path existed where the plan remained confidential longer, potentially allowing for back-channel modifications that might have been more palatable to Ukraine and Europe.\n",
    "is_polymarket": true,
    "is_overlap": false,
