    "id": "q_climate_20251231_008_acc11034",
    "title": "Will global carbon emissions from fossil fuels and industry in 2025 be lower than the previous year?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "Despite efforts to reduce emissions, the global output of greenhouse gases continued to be a major concern in 2025.",
    "resolution_criteria": "Reports from the Global Carbon Project or similar authoritative bodies confirm a decrease in global CO2 emissions from fossil fuels and industry in 2025 compared to 2024.",
    "outcome": "No",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nGlobal carbon emissions from fossil fuels and industry in 2025 did not decline but rather reached a record high of approximately 38.1 billion metric tons, increasing by roughly 1.1% from 2024. Despite significant deployments of renewable energy, absolute global energy demand grew at an unprecedented rate, overwhelming clean energy gains. A major driver was the surge in electricity demand from data centers, AI infrastructure, and cryptocurrency mining, which led to increased coal generation in markets like the United States. Additionally, entrenched political and industrial resistance, evidenced by the record presence of fossil fuel lobbyists at COP30 and policy rollbacks, prevented necessary structural declines in fossil fuel usage.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2025-09-15:** Reports confirm that U.S. carbon pollution rose significantly in the previous year, breaking a two-year downward trend, primarily due to increased power sector demands ([art_climate_20250915_005_98b1495f]).\n- **2025-10-15:** Deeper analysis into U.S. emissions reveals that surging power demand from AI data centers and crypto mining drove a resurgence in coal and natural gas use ([art_climate_20251015_004_b033c643]).\n- **2025-11-13:** The Global Carbon Budget officially projects fossil fuel CO2 emissions to hit a record high of 38.1 billion metric tons in 2025 ([art_general_20251113_009_bd85daf7], [art_general_20251113_007_3ca17a17]).\n- **2025-11-18:** During the COP30 climate talks in Brazil, it is revealed that a record number of fossil fuel lobbyists are in attendance, undermining efforts to negotiate a phase-out of fossil fuels ([art_general_20251118_016_df67fe39], [art_general_20251118_017_d068ebb8]).\n- **2025-12-05:** End-of-year assessments confirm that despite a massive global buildout of renewables, fossil fuel emissions set new records because overall energy demand outpaced clean generation ([art_climate_20251205_002_c729ebe7]).\n- **2025-12-18:** Tech giants like Google signal continued reliance on fossil fuels to power new data centers, highlighting the tension between tech expansion and climate goals ([art_general_20251218_012_96e0d9ce]).\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe root cause of the 2025 emissions increase was fundamentally an issue of **energy demand outpacing clean energy supply**. The rapid expansion of energy-intensive computing, specifically AI data centers and cryptocurrency mining, created an unexpected shock in electricity demand ([art_climate_20251015_004_b033c643]). Because renewable energy infrastructure could not scale fast enough to meet this localized demand, utility companies resorted to running existing coal and natural gas plants more frequently and delaying their retirement.\n\nThis dynamic triggered a near-term resurgence in fossil fuel combustion in advanced economies like the U.S., which had previously seen declining emissions. Furthermore, the intermediate mechanism of **entrenched fossil fuel interests** exacerbated the situation. At global summits like COP30, record lobbying numbers successfully blocked binding language on a fossil fuel phase-out ([art_general_20251118_016_df67fe39]). Consequently, without strong international policy mandates forcing a managed decline, and with baseline energy demand surging globally, the final outcome resulted in a record 38.1 billion metric tons of CO2 emissions in 2025 ([art_general_20251113_009_bd85daf7]).\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThere were significant forces pushing toward lower emissions. A massive global buildout of solar and wind capacity took place throughout 2024 and 2025. Additionally, dozens of individual nations did see their emissions peak and begin to decline ([art_general_20251114_014_13e50aa0]). However, these positive trends failed to reduce the global total because the absolute growth in global energy demand—driven by developing economies, extreme weather heating/cooling needs, and tech infrastructure—was simply larger than the total amount of new clean energy added to the grid.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1:** Surge in U.S. power demand due to AI data centers and crypto mining.\n  - **Date:** Mid-to-late 2025\n  - **Why it matters:** Reversed the U.S. trend of declining emissions by forcing increased reliance on coal and gas.\n  - **Source:** [art_climate_20251015_004_b033c643], [art_climate_20250915_005_98b1495f]\n- **E2:** Global Carbon Budget projects record high 38.1 billion tonnes.\n  - **Date:** 2025-11-13\n  - **Why it matters:** The definitive statistical confirmation of the negative resolution.\n  - **Source:** [art_general_20251113_009_bd85daf7], [art_general_20251113_007_3ca17a17]\n- **E3:** Record fossil fuel lobbyist presence at COP30 blocks phase-out.\n  - **Date:** 2025-11-18\n  - **Why it matters:** Prevented crucial international policy mechanisms that might have forced near-term emission cuts.\n  - **Source:** [art_general_20251118_016_df67fe39], [art_general_20251118_017_d068ebb8]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| U.S. emissions rose due to AI/crypto | [art_climate_20251015_004_b033c643], [art_climate_20250915_005_98b1495f] | 2025-10-15 | 0.95 | Key driver for reversing downward trends in advanced economies. |\n| Global Carbon Budget hits record | [art_general_20251113_009_bd85daf7], [art_general_20251113_007_3ca17a17] | 2025-11-13 | 0.99 | Definitive ground truth data. |\n| COP30 fossil fuel lobbying | [art_general_20251118_016_df67fe39], [art_general_20251118_017_d068ebb8] | 2025-11-18 | 0.90 | Explains the lack of policy response to rising emissions. |\n| Tech giants relying on fossil fuels | [art_general_20251218_012_96e0d9ce] | 2025-12-18 | 0.85 | Illustrates ongoing structural demand issues. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty going forward is whether the massive tech-driven energy demand is a temporary spike until localized clean energy (like advanced nuclear or next-gen geothermal) catches up, or a permanent structural shift. An alternative path could have occurred if global economic growth had slowed significantly in 2025, which historically is one of the few reliable mechanisms for short-term global emission drops.\n",
