    "id": "q_climate_20251231_083_7460fce5",
    "title": "How many acres of land will be burned by wildfires in the US in 2025?",
    "question_type": "quantity",
    "options": [
      "7500000"
    ],
    "background": "A quantitative measure of the impact of climate change on the US.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The total number of acres burned will be determined by the official figures released by the National Interagency Fire Center (NIFC) for the 2025 calendar year.",
    "outcome": "7500000",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe total area burned by wildfires in the US in 2025 reached approximately 7.5 million acres. The season was characterized by an extremely destructive start with the Los Angeles fires in January, driven by intense winds and dry conditions, followed by significant summer fires including the Grand Canyon megafire. Although massive property damage and loss of life occurred early on, the overall acreage burned nationwide by year-end settled at 7,500,000 acres, shaped by late-season weather patterns and federal suppression efforts amidst organizational shifts.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **January 2025**: Massive, wind-driven wildfires devastate Los Angeles (including the Palisades and Eaton fires), burning thousands of structures early in the year [art_general_20250109_024_5e221bea] [art_general_20250116_030_0b649fd7] [art_general_20250214_006_1af5368d].\n- **May - June 2025**: Debate surrounds federal forest and park service worker cuts and attempts to consolidate wildland fire agencies, complicating resource allocation ahead of peak season [art_general_20250508_011_9be98190] [art_general_20250629_009_a3e519a9].\n- **July - August 2025**: A major fire erupts near the Grand Canyon, escalating into a megafire that destroys historic structures [art_general_20250713_009_f569c27c] [art_general_20250801_007_352f654a].\n- **September - October 2025**: Elevated temperatures and winds spike wildfire risk again in Southern California, though total acreage nationwide remains somewhat controlled [art_general_20250905_003_8165c3ef] [art_general_20251028_022_92e479e2].\n- **November - December 2025**: The NIFC and NOAA summarize the year-end statistics, locking the national burned acreage at roughly 7.5 million acres [art_general_20251104_004_1aef77ad] [art_science_20251130_005_1cc339f3].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe severe January wildfires in Southern California, fueled by climate-aggravated conditions and intense Santa Ana winds, caused early devastation and set a high-alert tone for the year [art_general_20250128_023_e468534a]. Midway through the year, structural changes to wildland fire agencies and workforce cuts triggered challenges in early suppression capabilities [art_general_20250508_011_9be98190]. These vulnerabilities led to uncontained blazes in the summer, notably the Grand Canyon megafire [art_general_20250801_007_352f654a]. However, despite these high-profile, highly destructive fires, favorable weather in other regions during the fall resulted in a final national tally of 7.5 million acres burned, preventing an extreme record-breaking acreage year [art_general_20250905_003_8165c3ef].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nFederal budget shifts and organizational consolidations aimed at streamlining response theoretically pushed against widespread acreage growth, but faced implementation hurdles. Moreover, while California saw catastrophic damage in early 2025, parts of the country experienced milder conditions or active precipitation, holding the national acreage total below catastrophic historical peaks like 2020. \n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Los Angeles Wildfires Start\n  - Date: January 2025\n  - Why it matters: Sparked the 2025 season with mass destruction and human casualties, framing the national response.\n  - Source: [art_general_20250116_030_0b649fd7], [art_general_20250214_006_1af5368d]\n\n- E2: Wildland Fire Agency Consolidation\n  - Date: June 2025\n  - Why it matters: Altered the administrative and resource pipeline for federal fire suppression efforts.\n  - Source: [art_general_20250629_009_a3e519a9]\n\n- E3: Grand Canyon Megafire\n  - Date: July-August 2025\n  - Why it matters: Represented the peak of summer wildfire escalation, contributing heavily to regional acreage.\n  - Source: [art_general_20250801_007_352f654a]\n\n- E4: Year-End Reporting\n  - Date: November-December 2025\n  - Why it matters: Official confirmation of the 7.5 million acres total by NIFC/NOAA.\n  - Source: [art_science_20251130_005_1cc339f3]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Intense LA fires in January | [art_general_20250116_030_0b649fd7], [art_general_20250214_006_1af5368d] | Jan-Feb 2025 | 1.0 | 1,000+ structures destroyed |\n| Workforce cuts / agency restructuring | [art_general_20250508_011_9be98190], [art_general_20250629_009_a3e519a9] | May-June 2025 | 0.9 | Impacted suppression |\n| Grand Canyon megafire | [art_general_20250801_007_352f654a] | Aug 2025 | 1.0 | Major summer blaze |\n| Final year-end tally | [art_general_20251104_004_1aef77ad], [art_science_20251130_005_1cc339f3] | Nov 2025 | 1.0 | Confirms ~7.5M acres |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nEarly season forecasts feared well over 10 million acres might burn given the extreme January start. However, mild autumn weather in the Pacific Northwest and successful containment in key regions limited the total. Had fall winds in Northern California been more severe, the 7.5 million acre threshold could easily have been surpassed.\n",
