    "id": "q_climate_20260131_080_125ad9f7",
    "title": "Will the amount of renewable energy generated in the US in 2025 be higher than the amount generated in 2024?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "A question about the growth of renewable energy in the US.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The question will resolve to YES if the total amount of renewable energy generated in the US in 2025, as reported by the Energy Information Administration (EIA), is higher than the total amount generated in 2024. Otherwise, it will resolve to NO.",
    "outcome": "True",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe amount of renewable energy generated in the US in 2025 was higher than in 2024, resolving the question as True. This outcome was driven primarily by massive solar and wind capacity additions throughout 2024 and 2025, supported by incentives from the Inflation Reduction Act (IRA). Despite localized curtailment, supply chain constraints, and grid interconnection delays, the sheer volume of new renewable installations overwhelmingly increased total generation compared to the previous year.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2024 - 2025**: Extensive solar and wind generation capacity added to the US grid, incentivized by the IRA [art_science_20260115_039_43737133].\n- **Throughout 2025**: Total renewable energy generation ramps up as new projects come online, offsetting minor drops from localized grid curtailments.\n- **January 31, 2026**: Question resolution date confirms total 2025 renewable generation > 2024.\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n1. **Legislative Support (IRA)**: The Inflation Reduction Act provided significant financial incentives for renewable energy development.\n2. **Capacity Additions**: This triggered rapid deployment of new solar and wind farms.\n3. **Increased Generation**: As these new facilities connected to the grid and commenced operations in late 2024 and throughout 2025, total generation capacity surged.\n4. **Final Outcome**: The aggregate effect led to an undeniable year-over-year increase in renewable generation, causing the 2025 total to outpace 2024 [art_science_20260115_039_43737133].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\n- **Interconnection Delays & Curtailment**: Many regions (like CAISO and ERCOT) faced delays in grid connection and curtailment due to midday oversupply.\n- **Supply Chain Issues**: Intermittent supply chain bottlenecks slowed some project timelines.\n- *Why they failed*: The overwhelming scale of new capacity brought online far outweighed the generation lost to these constraints.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Record Solar and Wind Capacity Additions\n  - Date: 2024 - 2025\n  - Why it matters: Provided the foundational capacity required to increase total generation.\n  - Source: [art_science_20260115_039_43737133]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| IRA drove massive capacity additions leading to higher 2025 generation | art_science_20260115_039_43737133 | 2024-2025 | 0.9 | Strong consensus across broad collection |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\n- A severe nationwide weather anomaly (like prolonged wind drought or extreme snow covering solar panels broadly) could theoretically have depressed generation enough to fall below 2024 levels, but this did not occur.\n",
