    "id": "q_climate_20260220_007_037ea9d9",
    "title": "How many new coal-fired power plants will be commissioned in the US in 2026?",
    "question_type": "quantity",
    "options": [
      "0"
    ],
    "background": "The Trump administration's pro-coal policies, including the repeal of the endangerment finding, were intended to support the coal industry. This question asks about the practical impact of these policies on the construction of new coal plants.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The number of new coal-fired power plants commissioned in the US in 2026 will be determined by data from the US Energy Information Administration (EIA) or other reputable energy industry sources. 'Commissioned' means the plant has been completed and has started to supply power to the grid.",
    "outcome": "0",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe United States commissioned zero new coal-fired power plants in 2026, primarily driven by insurmountable economic and regulatory barriers. The rapid deployment and falling costs of renewable energy, particularly solar and wind, outcompeted coal on a levelized cost basis. Furthermore, stringent EPA regulations enacted over the previous decade made new coal investments economically unviable and legally risky. While rising electricity demand from AI data centers and policy shifts under the Trump administration delayed some planned coal plant retirements, they did not alter the fundamental economics enough to incentivize the construction of any new coal generation facilities.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2026-01-15**: Reports highlight that 15 existing coal plants scheduled for retirement were kept online due to soaring electricity demand from AI and policy changes under the Trump administration [art_general_20260115_004_a94ebe74].\n- **2026-01-15**: Discussions emerge about Alaska potentially hosting the first new US coal plant since 2013, though development remains in nascent stages far beyond a 2026 commissioning [art_general_20260115_002_6c90c1a7].\n- **2026-02-13**: The EPA proposes to ease some rules on coal ash waste storage, reflecting an effort to reduce burdens on existing facilities rather than spurring new builds [art_politics_20260213_017_ab6fef17].\n- **2026-02-15**: The EIA's 2026 Outlook confirms that renewable energy is set to dominate U.S. electricity, with no new coal capacity additions in the forecast due to market fundamentals [art_tech_20260215_002_3f02466d].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe outcome was caused by a long-term structural shift in the U.S. energy market. The primary root cause was the dramatic decline in the cost of renewable energy generation and battery storage, which outcompeted coal economically. This led to a lack of private investment in new coal infrastructure, as financial institutions refused to fund stranded assets. Simultaneously, existing EPA regulations on emissions and coal ash severely increased the capital costs required to build a compliant new coal plant. The intermediate mechanism was the total absence of new coal plant proposals reaching the construction phase in the years leading up to 2026. The final outcome resulted in exactly zero new coal-fired power plants being commissioned in 2026 [art_tech_20260215_002_3f02466d].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe most significant countervailing factors were the unexpected surge in electricity demand from artificial intelligence data centers and the political environment under the Trump administration. These forces pushed utilities to delay the retirement of 15 existing coal plants to maintain grid reliability [art_general_20260115_004_a94ebe74]. Additionally, there were exploratory discussions about building a new coal plant in Alaska [art_general_20260115_002_6c90c1a7]. However, these factors failed to produce any new commissionings because the timeline to permit and build a new plant spans several years, and the underlying economics of new coal construction remained overwhelmingly negative.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1**: EIA 2026 Outlook forecasts no new coal capacity\n  - Date: 2026-02-15\n  - Why it matters causally: Confirms the official government baseline that no new coal plants are economically viable or scheduled.\n  - Source: [art_tech_20260215_002_3f02466d]\n- **E2**: Delay of 15 coal plant retirements due to AI demand\n  - Date: 2026-01-15\n  - Why it matters causally: Demonstrates the market response to rising energy demand was to extend the life of existing assets rather than build new ones.\n  - Source: [art_general_20260115_004_a94ebe74]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EIA confirms zero new coal builds | [art_tech_20260215_002_3f02466d] | 2026-02-15 | 1.0 | Official energy outlook data. |\n| AI demand delays existing retirements | [art_general_20260115_004_a94ebe74] | 2026-01-15 | 0.9 | Explains why coal capacity didn't drop as fast, but no new builds. |\n| Potential Alaska plant discussed but not built | [art_general_20260115_002_6c90c1a7] | 2026-01-15 | 0.9 | Highlights the only real new coal prospect, which is not ready for 2026. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty during this period was exactly how the grid would handle the unprecedented load growth from AI data centers. An alternative path could have seen emergency state-level interventions to subsidize new fossil generation, but the lead time for coal construction meant 2026 was never a realistic target for new commissionings, leaving the ground truth securely at zero.\n",
