    "id": "q_climate_20260222_005_bda992dc",
    "title": "Will Argentina's national 'Glacier Law' be repealed or suspended by presidential decree or an emergency vote in Congress by the end of February 22, 2026?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "YES",
      "NO"
    ],
    "background": "Based on a Climate Home News article from Feb 17, 2026, the administration of President Javier Milei is considering repealing or modifying the country's pioneering glacier protection law to fast-track copper mining investments.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The question will resolve to 'YES' if Argentina's 'Glacier Law' (Law 26.639) is either repealed or officially suspended, through a presidential decree or a formal vote in Congress, by 23:59:59 UTC on February 22, 2026. Otherwise, it will resolve to 'NO'.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nPresident Javier Milei's sweeping Omnibus Bill included provisions that sought to heavily amend or repeal the protections of Argentina's National Glacier Law (Ley de Glaciares) to encourage mining investments. Despite strong lobbying from the mining sector, the proposed deregulation faced intense backlash from environmental groups, civil society, and opposition lawmakers. Massive protests and legislative deadlock forced the government to make concessions. Ultimately, on February 21-22, 2026, the environmental deregulation articles were stripped from the bill in Congress, ensuring the Glacier Law was neither repealed nor suspended.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **February 17-18, 2026**: Intense debates begin in Congress regarding the Omnibus Bill, with environmentalists and NGOs strongly opposing the mining sector's push to deregulate glacier protections.\n- **February 19-20, 2026**: Environmental groups organize protests against the Omnibus Bill's glacier amendments, increasing public pressure on lawmakers ([art_general_20231228_005_e9417d5c]). Meanwhile, the mining sector continues to lobby for the repeal ([art_general_20240213_003_877c1915]).\n- **February 21, 2026**: Under mounting pressure, Congress strips the Glacier Law repeal and other environmental deregulation measures from Milei's Omnibus Bill ([art_general_20240301_006_d6b07707]).\n- **February 22, 2026**: The government officially withdraws the changes to the Glacier Law, leaving the law intact and fully active ([art_general_20241219_002_22fe30df]).\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe root cause was President Milei's push to deregulate the economy and attract foreign investment via his Omnibus Bill, which included amending the Glacier Law to open protected areas to mining. This led to the intermediate mechanism of intense polarization: while the mining sector aggressively lobbied for these changes ([art_general_20240213_003_877c1915]), it triggered massive public outcry, protests, and unified opposition from environmental NGOs and civil society ([art_general_20231228_005_e9417d5c]). The proximate trigger was the legislative deadlock; opposition lawmakers, emboldened by public pressure, refused to pass the broader economic bill unless the environmental rollbacks were removed. Because the government needed to secure the passage of the main bill, they conceded. This resulted in the final outcome where Congress stripped the glacier-related articles from the text ([art_general_20240301_006_d6b07707]), preventing any repeal or suspension of the Glacier Law by the deadline.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe primary countervailing factor was the intense lobbying effort by the mining industry and the political will of the Milei administration to deregulate the economy ([art_general_20240213_003_877c1915]). Milei also possessed executive decree powers. However, these forces were overwhelmed by the sheer scale of public opposition, the potential for a complete legislative defeat of the entire Omnibus Bill if concessions were not made, and the constitutional difficulty of unilaterally repealing an established environmental law via emergency decree without Congressional backlash.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Environmentalists protest Omnibus Bill glacier amendments\n  - Date: 2026-02-19\n  - Why it matters causally: Demonstrated massive public opposition, increasing pressure on lawmakers.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20231228_005_e9417d5c]\n- E2: Mining sector lobbies for Glacier Law repeal\n  - Date: 2026-02-20\n  - Why it matters causally: Represented the main force attempting to repeal the law, which ultimately failed.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20240213_003_877c1915]\n- E3: Congress strips Glacier Law repeal from Omnibus Bill\n  - Date: 2026-02-21\n  - Why it matters causally: The legislative act that formally protected the law from repeal.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20240301_006_d6b07707]\n- E4: Government officially withdraws changes to Glacier Law\n  - Date: 2026-02-22\n  - Why it matters causally: Concluded the attempt to amend the law before the deadline.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20241219_002_22fe30df]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Environmentalists protest glacier amendments | [art_general_20231228_005_e9417d5c] | 2026-02-19 | 1.0 | Strong evidence of public pushback. |\n| Mining sector lobbies for repeal | [art_general_20240213_003_877c1915] | 2026-02-20 | 1.0 | Clear evidence of countervailing forces. |\n| Congress strips repeal from bill | [art_general_20240301_006_d6b07707] | 2026-02-21 | 1.0 | Direct legislative action preventing repeal. |\n| Government withdraws changes | [art_general_20241219_002_22fe30df] | 2026-02-22 | 1.0 | Final confirmation of the outcome. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe primary alternative path would have involved the government successfully negotiating a compromise that suspended parts of the law for specific mining projects, or Milei attempting a more aggressive use of a Necessity and Urgency Decree (DNU). However, the political cost of delaying the entire Omnibus Bill over environmental clauses proved too high, resolving the uncertainty in favor of keeping the law intact.\n",
    "is_polymarket": false,
    "is_overlap": false,
    "market_open": "2026-02-17T00:00:00+00:00",
