    "resolution_criteria": "The number will be determined by official press releases from the Department of Justice or the attorneys general of the respective states.",
    "outcome": "39",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe DOJ and numerous states sued Live Nation and Ticketmaster for antitrust violations due to anticompetitive practices in the live events market. The lawsuit initially included 29 states and the District of Columbia. In August 2024, 10 additional states joined the amended complaint, reflecting widespread bipartisan concern over ticketing monopolies. The total number of participating states reached 39 and remained at this figure through February 22, 2026.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **May 23, 2024**: The DOJ, along with 29 state attorneys general and the District of Columbia, file an antitrust lawsuit against Live Nation and Ticketmaster. [art_politics_20260220_002_bdad7182]\n- **August 2024**: The DOJ files an amended complaint adding 10 additional states (Indiana, Iowa, Kansas, Louisiana, Mississippi, Nebraska, New Mexico, South Dakota, Utah, and Vermont). [art_politics_20260220_003_99a5d83f]\n- **February 2026**: A jury rules against Live Nation/Ticketmaster, concluding the trial phase, with the total number of states remaining at 39. [art_politics_20260220_004_70e8e4c0]\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n- **Root Cause**: Live Nation and Ticketmaster's dominance in the live entertainment industry resulted in widespread consumer and political backlash over high fees and lack of competition. [art_politics_20260220_002_bdad7182]\n- **Intermediate Mechanism**: This backlash triggered an initial lawsuit by the DOJ and 29 states in May 2024. [art_politics_20260220_003_99a5d83f]\n- **Proximate Trigger**: The initial momentum led 10 more states to evaluate the lawsuit and join the amended complaint in August 2024. [art_politics_20260220_004_70e8e4c0]\n- **Final Outcome**: The number of participating states settled at 39, reflecting a strong bipartisan consensus. [art_politics_20260220_006_18061a51]\n\n## Countervailing Factors\n- **Live Nation's Defense**: Live Nation heavily lobbied and argued that their practices were standard industry procedures that did not harm consumers, which might have deterred some states from joining. However, the overwhelming public pressure and evidence of market power overcame these defenses.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1**: DOJ and 29 states file initial lawsuit against Live Nation\n  - **Date**: May 23, 2024\n  - **Why it matters causally**: Initiated the legal action.\n  - **Source article IDs**: [art_politics_20260220_002_bdad7182]\n- **E2**: 10 additional states join the lawsuit\n  - **Date**: August 2024\n  - **Why it matters causally**: Pushed the total state count to 39.\n  - **Source article IDs**: [art_politics_20260220_003_99a5d83f]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Initial lawsuit with 29 states | art_politics_20260220_002_bdad7182 | May 23, 2024 | 1.0 | DOJ announced lawsuit. |\n| 10 additional states join | art_politics_20260220_003_99a5d83f | August 2024 | 1.0 | Amended complaint filed. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\n- A minor uncertainty was whether additional states would join closer to the trial date, but the final count stabilized at 39 early in the legal process.\n",
