    "id": "q_culture_20260222_013_16159ae5",
    "title": "Will the Broadway League extend the cancellation of all shows beyond February 23, 2026?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "A major blizzard has forced the cancellation of Broadway shows for two consecutive days. The decision to reopen will depend on the severity and duration of the storm.",
    "resolution_criteria": "This question resolves to YES if the Broadway League announces that shows will remain closed on February 24th or later due to the blizzard. This will be determined by their official announcements.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe Broadway League did not extend the cancellation of all shows beyond February 23, 2026. The initial cancellations were triggered by a severe blizzard that struck New York City, creating hazardous travel conditions and prompting municipal warnings. However, the storm passed relatively quickly, allowing city services to clear the roads and public transit to resume normal operations. Consequently, the temporary safety measure was lifted, and Broadway performances resumed as scheduled after February 23.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **Late February 2026**: A massive winter storm approaches the Northeastern United States, prompting weather warnings for New York City [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912].\n- **February 22, 2026**: The blizzard hits NYC, causing hazardous conditions and travel bans. The Broadway League announces the cancellation of evening shows to ensure the safety of cast, crew, and patrons.\n- **February 23, 2026**: Cancellations continue through the day as the city digs out from the snow.\n- **Late February 23, 2026**: Weather conditions improve significantly, and travel bans are lifted. The Broadway League confirms that shows will resume the following day.\n- **February 24, 2026**: Broadway shows resume their regular schedules, ending the temporary shutdown.\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe root cause of the initial shutdown was an extreme weather event—a severe blizzard that hit New York City in late February 2026. This storm created dangerous conditions (intermediate mechanism), leading municipal authorities to issue travel advisories and bans (proximate trigger). Because it was unsafe for audiences and theater personnel to commute, the Broadway League canceled performances for February 22 and 23. However, the storm was short-lived. Once the weather cleared and city infrastructure was functional again, the safety concerns that necessitated the closure were eliminated. This rapid recovery led directly to the Broadway League deciding not to extend the cancellations beyond February 23, 2026, and performances resumed [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe primary countervailing factor was the potential for prolonged infrastructural damage or extended power outages caused by the blizzard, which could have forced a longer shutdown. Additionally, lingering snow and ice might have discouraged ticket holders from traveling to the theater district, creating an economic disincentive for reopening quickly. However, New York City's robust snow removal operations and the quick restoration of public transit mitigated these risks, allowing the theaters to reopen promptly.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1**: Severe blizzard hits New York City.\n  - Date: February 22, 2026\n  - Why it matters: The root cause of the initial theater closures.\n  - Source: [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912]\n- **E2**: Broadway League announces initial cancellations.\n  - Date: February 22, 2026\n  - Why it matters: The official action that stopped performances.\n  - Source: [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912]\n- **E3**: Broadway League confirms resumption of shows.\n  - Date: February 23, 2026\n  - Why it matters: The direct confirmation that the cancellation would not be extended.\n  - Source: [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Blizzard causes initial Broadway shutdown | [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912] | Feb 22, 2026 | 0.9 | Weather event triggered safety closures. |\n| Broadway resumes operations after Feb 23 | [art_culture_20260414_028_3dee5912] | Feb 24, 2026 | 0.9 | Rapid recovery allowed reopening. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty during the event was the exact duration and severity of the storm. Had the blizzard stalled over the city or caused significant damage to the power grid, the Broadway League would likely have been forced to extend the cancellations into late February. However, the swift passage of the storm system prevented this alternative path.\n",
