    "id": "q_culture_20260222_015_7cace754",
    "title": "How many Broadway shows will be forced to cancel performances on February 22 and 23, 2026?",
    "question_type": "quantity",
    "options": [
      "40"
    ],
    "background": "The Broadway League has announced a near-complete shutdown of all productions due to the blizzard.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The number will be determined by the official list of cancellations provided by the Broadway League.",
    "outcome": "40",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nA massive blizzard struck New York City and the Tri-State area on the weekend of February 22-23, 2026. This extreme winter weather event caused widespread transportation disruptions and led to local and state declarations of emergency. Consequently, the Broadway League and theater owners decided to cancel performances to ensure the safety of cast, crew, and audience members. The cancellations impacted Sunday evening and Monday shows, resulting in a total of 40 cancelled performances on Broadway.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **February 22, 2026**: A severe blizzard begins affecting the New York City region, producing heavy snowfall and whiteout conditions [art_general_20260222_032_8de09ce9, art_general_20260222_035_d9dcbcbc].\n- **February 22, 2026 (Evening)**: The Broadway League announces the cancellation of all Sunday evening shows due to the blizzard [art_culture_20260222_002_31efd247, art_general_20260222_031_a1f34b9d].\n- **February 23, 2026**: As the blizzard continues and a state of emergency remains in place, Monday night performances are also cancelled [art_culture_20260222_003_016e7794, art_general_20260222_033_09558d7d].\n- **February 23, 2026**: Governor Hochul seeks federal disaster assistance due to the storm's severity [art_general_20260223_011_f9c1db17].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n- **Root Cause**: A massive winter storm (the \"Blizzard of 2026\") struck the Northeastern US, centered over New York City, bringing severe snowfall [art_general_20260222_032_8de09ce9].\n- **Intermediate Mechanism**: The storm caused hazardous travel conditions, massive disruptions to public transportation, and led officials to declare a state of emergency [art_general_20260222_035_d9dcbcbc].\n- **Proximate Trigger**: Because performers, crew, and audiences could not safely travel to the theater district, theater owners and the Broadway League formally suspended operations for safety reasons [art_culture_20260222_002_31efd247].\n- **Final Outcome**: This sequence of events directly resulted in the cancellation of 40 scheduled Broadway performances across February 22 and 23 [art_culture_20260222_003_016e7794].\n\n## Countervailing Factors\n- **Financial Incentives**: The theater industry heavily relies on weekend performances for revenue, which typically pressures producers to keep shows running even in poor weather.\n- **The \"Show Must Go On\" Tradition**: Broadway has a long-standing tradition of attempting to perform despite adverse conditions. However, these forces were overwhelmed by the sheer severity of the blizzard and the mandatory travel restrictions imposed by the state of emergency.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Blizzard of 2026 hits New York City\n  - date: 2026-02-22 to 2026-02-23\n  - why it matters causally: The root cause weather event that made travel impossible.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20260222_032_8de09ce9, art_general_20260222_035_d9dcbcbc]\n- E2: State of Emergency Declared\n  - date: 2026-02-22\n  - why it matters causally: Official government action that restricted travel and provided the basis for widespread closures.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20260222_035_d9dcbcbc]\n- E3: Broadway League announces Sunday evening cancellations\n  - date: 2026-02-22\n  - why it matters causally: The proximate trigger for the first wave of cancellations.\n  - source article IDs: [art_culture_20260222_002_31efd247]\n- E4: Broadway League announces Monday cancellations\n  - date: 2026-02-23\n  - why it matters causally: The proximate trigger for the second wave of cancellations, reaching the final ground truth of 40 shows.\n  - source article IDs: [art_culture_20260222_003_016e7794, art_general_20260222_033_09558d7d]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|-------------|-------------|--------------|------------------|-------|\n| Severe blizzard strikes NYC | [art_general_20260222_032_8de09ce9, art_general_20260222_035_d9dcbcbc] | 2026-02-22 | 1.0 | Root cause of all disruptions |\n| State of Emergency declared | [art_general_20260222_035_d9dcbcbc] | 2026-02-22 | 1.0 | Hindered travel and operations |\n| Sunday evening shows cancelled | [art_culture_20260222_002_31efd247, art_general_20260222_031_a1f34b9d] | 2026-02-22 | 1.0 | First wave of closures |\n| Monday shows cancelled | [art_culture_20260222_003_016e7794, art_general_20260222_033_09558d7d] | 2026-02-23 | 1.0 | Second wave, resulting in 40 total cancellations |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\n- **Alternative Path**: If the storm had slightly missed the city or mostly produced rain, Broadway might have only seen a few scattered cancellations rather than a blanket closure.\n- **Uncertainty**: The exact breakdown of cancellations between Sunday matinees, Sunday evenings, and Monday evenings is not explicitly detailed, but the aggregate total was definitively reported as 40.\n\n",
