    "resolution_criteria": "The question will be resolved based on year-end 2025 data from the IMF or the Bank for International Settlements (BIS) on the currency composition of global oil transactions.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe share of global oil trades settled in non-USD currencies failed to exceed 25% in 2025 primarily due to the deeply entrenched nature of the US dollar in global financial infrastructure and the persistent strategic alignment between the United States and key oil producers. Despite speculative rumors surrounding the expiration of a purported \"petrodollar\" agreement with Saudi Arabia and active efforts by BRICS nations to promote local currency settlements, structural economic realities prevailed. High switching costs, Chinese capital controls, and the unparalleled liquidity of the dollar deterred a mass migration. Consequently, Saudi Arabia and other major exporters continued to price and settle the vast majority of their oil exports in US dollars.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **June 15, 2024**: Rumors circulate regarding Saudi Arabia canceling a historical \"petrodollar\" deal, sparking widespread speculation about imminent de-dollarization [art_finance_20240615_002_b8c46ab8].\n- **January 15, 2025**: Reports confirm that USD dominance in oil trade remains exceptionally strong at the start of the year, dismissing earlier panic [art_finance_20250115_006_6009bbad].\n- **July 15, 2025**: Numerous analyses of the petrodollar and potential shifts to the digital Yuan or BRICS currencies conclude that structural transitions are far slower than geopolitical rhetoric suggests [art_finance_20250715_006_cc1bb73f].\n- **August 15, 2025**: Financial experts issue warnings not to call time on dollar dominance yet, pointing to the structural hurdles facing alternative currencies [art_finance_20250815_007_37c070f5].\n- **October 8, 2025**: Assessments of BRICS initiatives to shift away from dollar dependence highlight persistent scaling issues and the lack of a unified, liquid alternative [art_politics_20251008_004_903c40a7].\n- **February 21, 2026**: The question resolves as False, confirming non-USD settlements did not breach the 25% threshold in 2025.\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe failure of de-dollarization in the oil market to reach the 25% threshold was driven by a clear causal chain. First, the structural dominance of the US dollar in global banking and trade finance resulted in substantial switching costs for any nation attempting to move away from it [art_finance_20250815_007_37c070f5]. This foundational reality meant that despite geopolitical motivations, the economic incentives favored the status quo. \n\nNext, attempts by BRICS nations to establish bilateral local currency trades were hindered by practical friction. Because China maintains strict capital controls and the Yuan lacks full convertibility, exporting nations accumulated localized currency reserves that were difficult to deploy globally [art_politics_20251008_004_903c40a7]. This caused key exporters, most notably Saudi Arabia, to resist a full pivot away from the dollar, as they required a liquid and universally accepted asset to fund their massive domestic infrastructure projects.\n\nFinally, the ongoing security relationship between the US and Saudi Arabia provided a crucial non-economic anchor. This security guarantee implicitly supported the financial status quo, leading to Saudi Arabia's continued reliance on USD invoicing [art_finance_20250115_006_6009bbad]. The combination of these factors triggered a self-reinforcing cycle where the dollar's network effects overwhelmed the limited inroads made by alternative currencies, keeping non-USD settlements well below 25%.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe most significant force pushing against the dollar was the political will of the BRICS+ coalition, particularly Russia and China, who actively sought to circumvent Western financial systems due to sanctions and strategic rivalry. Furthermore, the expiration of certain historical US-Saudi financial understandings [art_finance_20240615_002_b8c46ab8] provided a theoretical opening for a policy shift. However, these factors were overwhelmed because geopolitical ambition could not immediately replace the deep financial plumbing and liquidity provided by US Treasury markets.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1**: Circulation of rumors regarding the end of the Saudi petrodollar pact.\n  - title: Circulation of rumors regarding the end of the Saudi petrodollar pact\n  - date: June 2024\n  - why it matters: It served as the primary catalyst for speculation that the 25% threshold might be breached.\n  - source article IDs: [art_finance_20240615_002_b8c46ab8]\n- **E2**: BRICS struggles with currency settlement scale.\n  - title: BRICS struggles with currency settlement scale\n  - date: October 2025\n  - why it matters: Demonstrated the structural limits of the main alternative to the dollar.\n  - source article IDs: [art_politics_20251008_004_903c40a7]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Petrodollar pact rumors | [art_finance_20240615_002_b8c46ab8] | June 2024 | 0.9 | Sparked initial debate |\n| Continued USD dominance in early 2025 | [art_finance_20250115_006_6009bbad] | Jan 2025 | 0.9 | Reaffirmed baseline |\n| Structural hurdles for dollar alternatives | [art_finance_20250815_007_37c070f5] | Aug 2025 | 0.9 | Explains why transition failed |\n| BRICS shift limitations | [art_politics_20251008_004_903c40a7] | Oct 2025 | 0.85 | Details alternative failure |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty was whether a sudden geopolitical shock, such as a severe escalation in US-China trade relations or a breakdown in US-Saudi security ties, could force a rapid, non-economic decoupling. Had Saudi Arabia formally and aggressively shifted its pricing mechanism to a basket of currencies or the Yuan, the 25% threshold could have been breached in a cascading manner.\n",
