    "id": "q_health_20260131_057_249db930",
    "title": "What will be the global vaccination rate for measles in children by the end of 2025?",
    "question_type": "quantity",
    "options": [
      "85"
    ],
    "background": "A key indicator of global public health efforts.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The global vaccination rate for measles will be determined by the official figure released by the World Health Organization (WHO) for the 2025 calendar year.",
    "outcome": "85",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe global vaccination rate for the first dose of the measles vaccine in children stood at approximately 85% by the end of 2025. This rate remained stagnant and significantly below the 95% threshold required to achieve herd immunity. The failure to rebound to higher coverage levels was primarily driven by continued disruptions in health systems post-pandemic, an increase in vaccine hesitancy fueled by misinformation, and reductions in public health funding in vulnerable regions. These combined factors led to major outbreaks worldwide, further highlighting the consequences of the 85% coverage rate.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- 2025-02-07: Measles outbreak expands in West Texas around county with low vaccination rates, highlighting localized vulnerabilities [art_general_20250207_008_44efde61].\n- 2025-06-24: Reports indicate global vaccination efforts have stalled, leaving millions of children vulnerable [art_general_20250624_004_73f81ed2].\n- 2025-07-15: Global childhood vaccination shows only slight improvement, with major challenges remaining in reaching the 95% target [art_general_20250715_005_b384b4a5].\n- 2025-10-15: Analysis of the global resurgence of measles points to systemic health system failures and rising hesitancy [art_health_20251015_003_35c3025b].\n- 2025-11-20: UN reports global measles cases surge as 30 million children miss vaccines [art_health_20251120_002_69db1f57].\n- 2025-12-01: WHO warns of surging global measles cases due to falling and stagnating vaccination rates [art_general_20251201_004_51b27f79].\n- 2026-01-15: California sees the most measles cases in 7 years as disease resurges nationwide [art_health_20260115_009_9765ab3a].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe root cause of the 85% vaccination rate was the long-lasting impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on global health infrastructure, which created significant backlogs in routine immunization [art_general_20250624_004_73f81ed2]. This intermediate mechanism of weakened health systems was compounded by rising vaccine hesitancy and misinformation, which became more entrenched in many communities [art_health_20251015_003_35c3025b]. The proximate trigger for the stalled recovery was inadequate funding and the slow rollout of catch-up campaigns, which failed to reach enough children to significantly boost the global average [art_health_20251120_002_69db1f57]. This led to the final outcome of an 85% vaccination rate and subsequent severe global outbreaks.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nEfforts like the \"Big Catch-Up\" campaign and interventions by organizations such as WHO, UNICEF, and Gavi attempted to rapidly increase vaccination coverage. These initiatives aimed to close the immunity gap and restore rates to pre-pandemic levels. However, they were overwhelmed by the sheer volume of missed doses, logistical challenges in low-income countries, and the strong headwind of growing anti-vaccine sentiment in high-income regions.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1\n  - title: Reports of stalled global vaccination efforts.\n  - date: 2025-06-24\n  - why it matters causally: Confirmed that post-pandemic recovery was not progressing as needed to reach targets.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20250624_004_73f81ed2]\n- E2\n  - title: UN report on 30 million children missing vaccines.\n  - date: 2025-11-20\n  - why it matters causally: Provided hard data on the massive scale of the immunization gap driving the outcome.\n  - source article IDs: [art_health_20251120_002_69db1f57]\n- E3\n  - title: WHO warning on surging global measles cases.\n  - date: 2025-12-01\n  - why it matters causally: Solidified the connection between the stagnant vaccination rate and the resulting global health crisis just before the resolution date.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20251201_004_51b27f79]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stalled global vaccination efforts | [art_general_20250624_004_73f81ed2] | 2025-06-24 | 0.9 | Showed recovery from pandemic lows had halted. |\n| 30 million children miss vaccines | [art_health_20251120_002_69db1f57] | 2025-11-20 | 0.95 | Quantified the missed population. |\n| WHO warning on surging cases due to low rates | [art_general_20251201_004_51b27f79] | 2025-12-01 | 0.95 | Directly linked the 85% rate to the ongoing outbreaks. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nIt remains slightly uncertain exactly how much of the stagnation was due to structural health system deficits versus active vaccine hesitancy, as the balance varies heavily by region. An alternative path where the \"Big Catch-Up\" campaigns were more generously funded might have seen global coverage edge closer to 88-90%, but the systemic barriers proved too high within the 2025 timeframe.\n",
