    "id": "q_health_20260228_001_b181b1f7",
    "title": "Will the FDA have approved Merck's belzutifan for advanced renal cell carcinoma by February 2026?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "Merck is developing belzutifan, a drug for advanced renal cell carcinoma. The drug has shown promising results in clinical trials.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The FDA's official announcement on the approval of Merck's belzutifan for the specified indication will be used to resolve this question.",
    "outcome": "True",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe FDA approved Merck's belzutifan (Welireg) for the treatment of advanced renal cell carcinoma (RCC) following prior therapies in December 2023. This approval was triggered by positive clinical trial results showing the drug's efficacy in treating advanced RCC in patients who had previously received PD-1/PD-L1 inhibitors and a VEGF-TKI. Thus, the early success of the clinical data led to the question of whether the FDA will have approved Merck's belzutifan for advanced renal cell carcinoma by February 2026 resolving as True, as the approval already occurred well before the deadline.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **December 14, 2023**: The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) approved Merck's drug belzutifan for a type of kidney cancer, specifically advanced renal cell carcinoma [art_general_20231214_004_f3df2c95].\n- **December 15, 2023**: Further coverage confirmed that the FDA approved Welireg (belzutifan) for patients with advanced renal cell carcinoma following PD-1 or PD-L1 inhibitor and a VEGF-TKI therapy [art_general_20231215_002_1c253382].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe outcome was driven by successful clinical trials demonstrating the safety and efficacy of belzutifan in the target patient population.\n1. Merck developed belzutifan (Welireg), a hypoxia-inducible factor-2 alpha inhibitor.\n2. Clinical trials demonstrated the efficacy of belzutifan in patients with advanced renal cell carcinoma who had previously received a PD-1 or PD-L1 inhibitor and a VEGF-TKI.\n3. The positive trial results caused Merck to submit an application for FDA approval.\n4. The FDA reviewed the clinical data and determined the benefits outweighed the risks. This resulted in the regulatory approval of Welireg for advanced RCC in December 2023 [art_general_20231214_004_f3df2c95]. This completely satisfies the resolution criteria of the question long before the February 2026 target date.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThere were no significant countervailing factors that prevented the approval, as the drug's efficacy profile in the previously treated advanced RCC population was strong enough to secure FDA authorization. Earlier failures or uncertainties in different combinations or settings (such as triplet combinations) did not derail the core approval for this indication.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: FDA approves Merck's belzutifan (Welireg) for advanced renal cell carcinoma.\n  - date: 2023-12-14\n  - why it matters causally: This is the exact event the question is asking about, firmly resolving the outcome to True.\n  - source article IDs: [art_general_20231214_004_f3df2c95], [art_general_20231215_002_1c253382]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FDA approves belzutifan for advanced RCC | art_general_20231214_004_f3df2c95, art_general_20231215_002_1c253382 | 2023-12-14 | 1.0 | Clear and unambiguous FDA approval announcement. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nGiven that the approval occurred in late 2023, there were no remaining uncertainties regarding this specific milestone. Alternative paths, such as expanded approvals in combination with other therapies (e.g., lenvatinib) or in earlier lines of treatment, continued to be explored by Merck, but the baseline approval for advanced RCC was already secured.\n",
