    "outcome": "True",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nIn early 2026, an EU member state successfully signed a long-term energy deal with a non-US supplier, specifically stating that this was necessary for diversification purposes. The drive to move away from Russian energy reliance and mitigate risks from volatile US energy supplies led to this agreement. This directly caused the positive resolution of the question.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **February 2026**: High-level visits and negotiations between the EU and suppliers like Algeria, emphasizing diversification from Russian gas [art_web_20260210_001_1471719b].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n- **Root Cause**: The continuous desire of the European Union to completely phase out Russian gas caused a massive shift in their energy strategy.\n- **Intermediate Mechanisms**: This shift led to the EU actively engaging non-US suppliers such as Algeria and Qatar throughout 2025 and early 2026 to secure long-term LNG and pipeline contracts.\n- **Proximate Triggers**: Intensive negotiations and a need to reduce US dependency triggered the finalization of new deals.\n- **Final Outcome**: An EU member state signed a definitive long-term energy deal in early 2026 with a non-US supplier, explicitly citing diversification, which resulted in the question resolving positively.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\n- The US remains a major LNG supplier to Europe; however, Europe specifically sought non-US sources to avoid over-reliance.\n- Volatility in the Middle East threatened LNG shipments from Qatar, but alternative routes or suppliers (like Algeria or Norway) were pursued instead.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: EU-Algeria energy talks\n  - title: EU-Algeria energy talks\n  - date: Feb 2026\n  - why it matters causally: Showcased the active engagement of EU with non-US suppliers.\n  - source article IDs: [art_politics_20260101_002_d9ef541f]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EU-Algeria talks | art_politics_20260101_002_d9ef541f | Feb 2026 | 0.9 | Strong evidence of diversification efforts. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nIt remains slightly uncertain if earlier deals in 2025 also explicitly cited diversification in exactly the same terminology, but the 2026 deal solidifies the outcome.\n",
