    "id": "q_politics_20260220_006_5448274f",
    "title": "Will the US federal government regulate greenhouse gas emissions from power plants by the end of 2026?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "Following the repeal of the 'endangerment finding', the legal basis for federal regulation of greenhouse gases was weakened. This question asks whether new regulations for power plant emissions will be implemented despite this.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The US federal government will be considered to be regulating greenhouse gas emissions from power plants if the EPA finalizes and begins enforcement of a rule that sets new, more stringent limits on CO2 emissions from existing or new power plants.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe US federal government failed to regulate greenhouse gas emissions from power plants by the end of 2026 primarily due to the Environmental Protection Agency's (EPA) reversal of its core regulatory mandate. In February 2026, the EPA finalized the rescission of the 2009 \"Endangerment Finding,\" which was the legal and statutory basis for the agency to regulate greenhouse gases as pollutants under the Clean Air Act. This deregulatory action effectively removed the federal government's jurisdiction to mandate emission reductions from power plants. The ensuing multi-state litigation against the repeal was not resolved before the 2026 deadline, locking in the lack of federal regulation.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- December 2009: The EPA issues the original \"Endangerment Finding,\" classifying greenhouse gases as pollutants under the Clean Air Act.\n- February 12, 2026: The EPA under Administrator Lee Zeldin rescinds the \"Endangerment Finding,\" officially determining that the agency lacks statutory authority to regulate greenhouse emissions to address global climate change. [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n- February 2026: Immediate backlash and legal challenges begin organizing among states and environmental groups in response to the EPA's rollback. [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n- March 19, 2026: A coalition of over 20 states, led by Massachusetts and California, files a massive lawsuit challenging the EPA's repeal of the Endangerment Finding. [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n1. Root Cause: The Trump administration and the EPA, driven by a deregulatory agenda, sought to remove federal constraints on the fossil fuel energy sector to promote economic growth.\n2. Intermediate Mechanism: To achieve this, the EPA targeted the legal foundation of its own regulatory power—the 2009 Endangerment Finding. By rescinding this finding, the EPA eliminated its statutory obligation and authority under the Clean Air Act to regulate greenhouse gases. [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n3. Proximate Trigger: The finalization of the repeal on February 12, 2026, stripped the agency of its power to impose greenhouse gas regulations on power plants, as it explicitly concluded \"it lacks statutory authority to regulate (greenhouse) emissions in response to global climate change concerns.\" [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n4. Final Outcome: Without the statutory authority provided by the Endangerment Finding, the federal government could not enact or enforce any new regulations on power plant emissions before the 2026 deadline.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe primary force pushing against this outcome was the legal and political resistance from Democratic-led states and environmental advocacy groups. Over 20 states, recognizing the environmental and health impacts of greenhouse gases, launched a multi-state lawsuit on March 19, 2026, to reinstate the Endangerment Finding. [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f] However, this factor failed to change the outcome because the lawsuit process is slow and complex; it could not produce a binding legal reversal before the end of 2026.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1\n  - title: EPA issues final rule rescinding the 2009 Greenhouse Gas Endangerment Finding\n  - date: February 12, 2026\n  - why it matters causally: This removed the legal authority for the EPA to regulate greenhouse gas emissions, fundamentally preventing federal regulation of power plants.\n  - source article IDs: [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n- E2\n  - title: Coalition of over 20 states files lawsuit against EPA over repeal of Endangerment Finding\n  - date: March 19, 2026\n  - why it matters causally: Represents the major legal challenge to the repeal, although it was not resolved in time to force regulation by the deadline.\n  - source article IDs: [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| EPA rescinds Endangerment Finding on Feb 12, 2026 | [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f] | 2026-02-12 | 1.0 | Directly quoted the ruling date in the article. |\n| States file lawsuit on March 19 | [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f] | 2026-03-19 | 1.0 | Article explicitly names the date and key states involved. |\n| EPA cites lack of statutory authority under Clean Air Act | [art_politics_20260215_003_83fcdd5f] | 2026-02-12 | 1.0 | Explicitly quoted from the EPA ruling. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main uncertainty lies in the outcome of the multi-state lawsuit. An alternative path could have occurred if a federal court had issued an emergency injunction immediately halting the repeal, forcing the EPA to maintain the finding and continue regulating emissions. However, given the conservative leaning of the federal judiciary and the complex administrative law involved, a rapid injunction was highly unlikely, cementing the negative outcome.\n",
