    "id": "q_politics_20260220_015_9cee5eac",
    "title": "Will the 2026 Munich Security Report rank climate change as a top 5 risk for G7 nations?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "In 2021, climate change was the top risk for G7 nations in the Munich Security Report's survey. However, by 2026, it had slipped to sixth place. This question asks if it will re-enter the top 5.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The question will be resolved based on the 2026 Munich Security Report. If climate change is listed among the top 5 risks for G7 nations in the report's survey, the answer is YES. Otherwise, it is NO.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "## Executive Summary\nThe 2026 Munich Security Report (MSR) did not rank climate change as a top 5 risk for G7 nations because shifting geopolitical and digital landscapes drastically altered threat perceptions. Faced with immediate political crises, shifting US foreign policy, and escalating digital warfare, G7 populations prioritized more immediate disruptions. Consequently, cyberattacks, economic crises, and disinformation emerged as the preeminent threats, causing climate change and environmental risks to fall out of the top 5 rankings for the G7.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **Late 2025 - Early 2026**: A noticeable shift in US foreign policy and domestic political climates increases the perceived risk of trade wars and democratic breakdown, shifting the global focus toward immediate political instability [art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38].\n- **February 13, 2026**: The Munich Security Conference publishes the \"Munich Security Index 2026\" as part of the \"Under Destruction\" MSR. Data reveals a divergence: while BICS nations still rank environmental risks highest, G7 nations deprioritize them [art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38].\n- **February 13-14, 2026**: Global news outlets report that cyberattacks have dominated the G7 risk hierarchy for the second consecutive year, pushing environmental concerns further down the list. Think tanks and policy forums begin lamenting the diminished priority of climate security [art_tech_20260214_008_500c7372, art_tech_20260214_014_e23f81f4, art_climate_20260213_005_6b51f978].\n- **February 20, 2026**: Final question resolution occurs as it becomes an established fact that climate change is completely excluded from the top 5 G7 risks in the 2026 report.\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe primary cause of the outcome was the dramatic rise in immediate, disruptive political and digital threats (like trade wars and US policy instability) which overshadowed long-term systemic risks. This shift caused respondents in G7 countries to pivot away from climate concerns, despite rising actual costs of global warming [art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38]. This pivot resulted in cyberattacks, economic/financial crises, and disinformation solidifying their spots as the paramount threats to G7 countries [art_tech_20260214_014_e23f81f4]. Because of this intense focus on immediate disruption, the Munich Security Index 2026 ultimately relegated climate change and environmental risks below the top 5 for G7 nations, finalizing the negative outcome for the question.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe actual, accelerating material costs of global warming, such as extreme weather events and forest fires, continuously put upward pressure on climate risk perceptions [art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38]. Furthermore, advocacy from organizations like Chatham House argued strongly that climate security should be a core priority [art_climate_20260213_005_6b51f978]. However, these factors were completely overwhelmed by the urgency of acute political and digital threats, leading G7 respondents to view climate issues as less \"imminent.\" Interestingly, these factors were enough to keep environmental risks at the #1 spot for BICS nations, but not for the G7.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: US foreign policy shifts and political instability escalate global focus on trade wars and immediate disruptions.\n  - Date: Late 2025 - Early 2026\n  - Why it matters: Set the geopolitical backdrop that overshadowed climate risks.\n  - Source article IDs: art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38\n- E2: Publication of the Munich Security Index 2026.\n  - Date: 2026-02-13\n  - Why it matters: Officially documented the shift in risk perception, confirming cyber threats as the #1 G7 risk and removing climate change from the top 5.\n  - Source article IDs: art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38, art_tech_20260214_014_e23f81f4\n- E3: Policy forums emphasize the demotion of climate security.\n  - Date: 2026-02-13\n  - Why it matters: Highlights the policy community's reaction to the explicit exclusion of climate from the most urgent threats.\n  - Source article IDs: art_climate_20260213_005_6b51f978\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n| ----------- | ----------- | ------------ | ---------------- | ----- |\n| Climate change fell out of top 5 G7 risks | art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38, art_tech_20260214_014_e23f81f4 | 2026-02-13 | 1.0 | Core resolution data explicitly found in the MSI 2026 publication. |\n| Cyberattacks dominate G7 concerns | art_tech_20260214_014_e23f81f4, art_tech_20260214_008_500c7372 | 2026-02-14 | 1.0 | Cyber threats are explicitly stated as the #1 risk. |\n| Perceived imminence of environmental risks dropped despite actual warming costs | art_politics_20260213_002_27b42a38 | 2026-02-13 | 1.0 | BICS nations still ranked them top, but G7 saw a steady decline. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nWhile the shift was decisive for the 2026 report, an alternative path might have unfolded if a catastrophic climate event had occurred precisely in late 2025 or early 2026, which could have spiked immediate risk perception. However, the dominance of \"wrecking-ball politics\" and acute digital vulnerabilities cemented the trajectory away from long-term environmental focus for the G7.\n",
