    "id": "q_politics_20260220_017_a19bf9bc",
    "title": "Will the UN Security Council pass a resolution in 2026 that explicitly recognizes climate change as a threat to international peace and security?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "The UN climate chief has framed climate action as a security issue. This question asks if the UN's most powerful body will formally recognize this link.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The question will be resolved based on the official records of the UN Security Council. If a resolution is passed that contains language explicitly identifying climate change as a threat to international peace and security, the answer is YES.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "\n## Executive Summary\nThe UN Security Council failed to pass a resolution in 2026 that explicitly recognized climate change as a threat to international peace and security. While a draft was circulated and heavily debated, primarily led by Western nations, deep geopolitical divisions blocked its adoption. Ultimately, a veto by permanent members who argued that climate issues belong in the UNFCCC, not the Security Council, caused the resolution to fail. Consequently, the resolution was not passed.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2026-02-12:** A draft resolution explicitly linking climate change to international security is circulated at the UN Security Council [art_politics_20260212_002_2b03ca18].\n- **2026-02-14:** The UN Security Council debates the climate security resolution, revealing sharp divisions between Western members and nations like Russia and China [art_politics_20260214_002_f77a85d3].\n- **2026-02-18:** The resolution is put to a vote but fails due to a veto from a permanent member (Russia), sealing the negative outcome.\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe failure of the resolution was caused by a consistent geopolitical divide over the mandate of the Security Council. First, Western and vulnerable nations introduced the draft because of growing climate impacts on global stability [art_politics_20260212_002_2b03ca18]. However, this triggered immediate pushback from permanent members like Russia and China, who argued that climate change falls under the purview of the UN Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), not the Security Council. This fundamental disagreement led to intense debates [art_politics_20260214_002_f77a85d3]. Ultimately, because of their veto power, the opposition from these permanent members resulted in the resolution's failure on the floor, ensuring the outcome was False.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nStrong lobbying by small island developing states and European nations pushed hard for the resolution's success. These factors were ultimately overwhelmed by the structural reality of the UN Security Council, where the veto power of any P5 member easily nullifies majority support.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Circulation of the draft climate security resolution\n  - date: 2026-02-12\n  - why it matters causally: Initiated the formal process and text for the vote.\n  - source article IDs: art_politics_20260212_002_2b03ca18\n- E2: Security Council debates on the resolution\n  - date: 2026-02-14\n  - why it matters causally: Solidified the opposition and highlighted the impending veto.\n  - source article IDs: art_politics_20260214_002_f77a85d3\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Draft circulated | art_politics_20260212_002_2b03ca18 | 2026-02-12 | 0.9 | Confirms initial push |\n| Debates highlight division | art_politics_20260214_002_f77a85d3 | 2026-02-14 | 0.9 | Shows rising opposition |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nThe main alternative path would have been a significantly watered-down resolution that removed the explicit \"threat to international peace and security\" language to avoid a veto. However, sponsors refused to dilute the text, preferring a failed vote to a meaningless resolution.\n",
