    "id": "q_politics_20260220_030_82a31ce7",
    "title": "Will the US government impose new sanctions on Venezuela's oil industry in 2026?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "The article mentions the US's 'de facto control' over Venezuela's oil industry. This question asks if the US will use its power to impose new sanctions in 2026.",
    "resolution_criteria": "The question will be resolved based on official announcements from the US Treasury Department's Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC). If OFAC issues new sanctions specifically targeting Venezuela's oil industry in 2026, the answer is YES.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "\n# Executive Summary\nThe US government did not impose new sanctions on Venezuela's oil industry in early 2026 due to a dramatic shift in policy following the arrest of President Nicolas Maduro in a U.S. military operation. The Trump administration, advised by American oil executives, pivoted from maximum pressure to engagement with interim President Delcy Rodriguez. Seeking to revive Venezuela's oil infrastructure and secure favorable contracts for US companies, the administration prioritized diplomatic and economic cooperation over new punitive measures.\n\n# Timeline Of Key Events\n- Jan 8, 2026: It is reported that U.S. forces arrested Nicolas Maduro in a military operation, leaving Delcy Rodriguez as interim President [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637].\n- Jan 8, 2026: Billionaire oil magnate Harry Sargeant III and other executives advise the Trump administration to work with Rodriguez to revive the oil sector rather than impose sanctions [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637].\n- Feb 12, 2026: Trump touts good relations with Venezuela [art_general_20260212_010_56f56020].\n- Feb 20, 2026: The resolution window closes with no new sanctions implemented.\n\n# Causal Chain Analysis\nThe root cause was the successful U.S. military operation that arrested President Nicolas Maduro [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637]. This led to a power vacuum filled by Delcy Rodriguez. The intermediate mechanism was the Trump administration's decision to consult with U.S. oil executives, such as Harry Sargeant III, who advised that Rodriguez would be amenable to transitioning the country and securing favorable contracts for American oil companies [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637]. This triggered a pivot in U.S. policy away from sanctions and towards economic collaboration. The final outcome was that no new sanctions were imposed by the February 20, 2026 deadline, as the U.S. focused on rebuilding Venezuela's oil infrastructure [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637].\n\n# Countervailing Factors\nThe historical momentum of the \"maximum pressure\" campaign from previous years pushed toward more sanctions. However, this was overwhelmed by the sudden regime change (Maduro's arrest) and the immediate lobbying by U.S. oil interests to seize the new opportunity for investment and crude exports.\n\n# Event Candidate Inventory\n- **E1** Maduro Arrested\n  - Date: Early January 2026\n  - Why it matters: Removed the primary target of U.S. sanctions, opening the door to new relations.\n  - Source: [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637]\n- **E2** Oil Executives Advise Trump\n  - Date: Jan 8, 2026\n  - Why it matters: Pushed the administration toward cooperation rather than sanctions.\n  - Source: [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637]\n- **E3** Trump Touts Good Relations\n  - Date: Feb 12, 2026\n  - Why it matters: Confirmed the positive diplomatic stance during the critical window.\n  - Source: [art_general_20260212_010_56f56020]\n\n# Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Maduro arrested | [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637] | Jan 8, 2026 | 1.0 | Key turning point |\n| Trump administration advised to work with Rodriguez | [art_general_20260108_013_720fe637] | Jan 8, 2026 | 1.0 | Shift in policy |\n| Trump touts good relations | [art_general_20260212_010_56f56020] | Feb 12, 2026 | 0.9 | Context |\n\n# Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nIt is uncertain if the positive relations will last long-term if Rodriguez does not transition the country to democracy as expected. An alternative path would have been the U.S. rejecting Rodriguez and doubling down on sanctions to force Machado into power.\n",
