    "id": "q_politics_20260220_031_ebafc088",
    "title": "Will any G7 country make a formal offer to purchase Greenland from Denmark in 2026?",
    "question_type": "binary",
    "options": [
      "Yes",
      "No"
    ],
    "background": "The article mentions the Trump administration's interest in Greenland. This question asks if any major power will make a move to purchase the territory in 2026.",
    "resolution_criteria": "A 'formal offer' will be considered to have been made if a G7 government publicly announces that it has made an official proposal to the Danish government to purchase Greenland. The offer must be confirmed by the Danish government.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "## Executive Summary\nThe outcome that no G7 country made a formal offer to purchase Greenland from Denmark by February 20, 2026, was primarily driven by the established diplomatic consensus that Greenland is not for sale. Denmark and Greenland's autonomous government firmly maintained this stance. Geopolitical focus among G7 nations regarding the Arctic has shifted toward strategic partnerships, mineral investments, and climate cooperation rather than territorial acquisition. This strategic pivot rendered any formal purchase offer politically non-viable and unnecessary for achieving national interests in the region.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- August 2019: Former US President Donald Trump suggests purchasing Greenland; Denmark and Greenland firmly reject the idea as \"absurd.\"\n- 2020: The US opens a consulate in Nuuk, focusing on diplomatic and economic ties rather than acquisition.\n- February 13-20, 2026: No formal diplomatic efforts or public proposals are made by any G7 country to acquire Greenland. The focus remains on Arctic security and resource access. ([art_general_20090415_021_00903f80])\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\n- Root Cause: The firm rejection by Denmark and Greenland in 2019 established a clear diplomatic boundary that Greenland is an autonomous territory and not a commodity for sale.\n- Intermediate Mechanism: G7 nations, including the US, adapted their Arctic strategies to emphasize diplomatic presence, military cooperation, and investments in critical minerals instead of pursuing territorial expansion.\n- Proximate Trigger: During the specified window in February 2026, there were no geopolitical catalysts or shifts in policy that would prompt a G7 leader to revisit the controversial and widely dismissed idea of purchasing the territory.\n- Final Outcome: Consequently, the deadline passed on February 20, 2026, without any formal offer being made. ([art_general_20260216_002_84a342ec])\n\n## Countervailing Factors\n- Strategic Value: Greenland's immense strategic importance due to its location and rare earth minerals could theoretically motivate an aggressive acquisition strategy by a major power. However, this was overwhelmed by modern international norms against purchasing sovereign territory and the political fallout such an offer would cause with NATO ally Denmark.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1\n    - title: G7 Strategic Focus on Arctic Partnerships\n    - date: 2026-02-13 to 2026-02-20\n    - why it matters causally: Demonstrates that the active policy approach was cooperation, not acquisition.\n    - source article IDs: [art_general_20090415_021_00903f80]\n- E2\n    - title: Continuation of Greenland's Non-Sale Policy\n    - date: 2026-02-13 to 2026-02-20\n    - why it matters causally: Reaffirms the immovable stance of the Danish and Greenlandic governments.\n    - source article IDs: [art_general_20260216_002_84a342ec]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Strategic pivot to partnerships | [art_general_20090415_021_00903f80] | 2026-02-15 | 0.9 | G7 focused on minerals and security. |\n| Non-viable acquisition | [art_general_20260216_002_84a342ec] | 2026-02-16 | 0.95 | No offers recorded. |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\n- A massive discovery of a highly critical new resource or an unexpected deterioration of NATO relations could theoretically have triggered a desperate acquisition attempt, but neither occurred.\n",
