    "resolution_criteria": "The question will resolve to YES if a new men's 100-meter dash world record is officially ratified by World Athletics in 2025. Otherwise, it will resolve to NO.",
    "outcome": "False",
    "explanation": "## Executive Summary\nThe highly anticipated 2025 athletic season did not produce a new men's 100-meter world record, leaving Usain Bolt's historic 9.58s mark intact. While several top sprinters showed exceptional form throughout the year, none were able to string together the perfect combination of peak fitness, ideal track conditions, and flawless execution required to break the 16-year-old record. The suspension of key competitor Fred Kerley and the tactical nature of major championship races further hindered any record-breaking attempts. Ultimately, Oblique Seville claimed the World Championship title, upstaging favorites like Noah Lyles, but finishing well outside world record territory.\n\n## Timeline Of Key Events\n- **2025-06-28**: Kishane Thompson runs a blazing time, achieving the best 100m time since Bolt's retirement, yet still falling short of the 9.58s record [art_general_20250628_018_f38232af].\n- **2025-08-13**: American sprinter Fred Kerley is provisionally suspended for anti-doping ‘whereabouts failures’, removing a top contender from the circuit [art_general_20250813_028_f4db194f].\n- **2025-09-14**: At the 2025 World Athletics Championships in Tokyo, Oblique Seville upstages Noah Lyles to win the 100m gold, but the winning time does not challenge the world record [art_general_20250914_019_a383968e].\n\n## Causal Chain Analysis\nThe failure to establish a new world record in 2025 was the result of a combination of physical limits and situational factors. First, the 9.58s record represents an extraordinary physiological benchmark that requires flawless mechanics and environmental conditions. Although athletes like Kishane Thompson showed promise early in the season (running sub-9.80s) [art_general_20250628_018_f38232af], maintaining that level of peak performance into the championship season proved difficult. Furthermore, the suspension of major rival Fred Kerley [art_general_20250813_028_f4db194f] reduced the competitive depth that often pushes athletes to faster times. Finally, major events like the World Athletics Championships prioritized tactical racing and medal acquisition over pure time trialing. This culminated in Oblique Seville's victory in Tokyo [art_general_20250914_019_a383968e], a race won on competitive merit rather than record-breaking speed, sealing the outcome that the record would survive 2025.\n\n## Countervailing Factors\nThe main countervailing force was the overall depth and speed of the current generation of sprinters. Noah Lyles, coming off a stellar 2024 Olympic campaign, and Kishane Thompson, who posted incredibly fast times in mid-2025, created a strong narrative that the record might be vulnerable. Advances in spike technology and track surfaces also provided theoretical advantages. However, these forces failed to overcome the sheer magnitude of Bolt's outlier performance from 2009.\n\n## Event Candidate Inventory\n- E1: Kishane Thompson posts best 100m time since Bolt's retirement\n  - Date: 2025-06-28\n  - Why it matters causally: Demonstrated the peak speed of the 2025 season but established that even the fastest times were still far off the WR.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20250628_018_f38232af]\n- E2: Fred Kerley suspended for whereabouts failures\n  - Date: 2025-08-13\n  - Why it matters causally: Removed a top-tier competitor, reducing the elite depth necessary to push the field to historic times.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20250813_028_f4db194f]\n- E3: Oblique Seville wins 100m at Tokyo World Championships\n  - Date: 2025-09-14\n  - Why it matters causally: Confirmed that the season's premier event yielded a champion but no world record, sealing the negative outcome for the year.\n  - Source article IDs: [art_general_20250914_019_a383968e]\n\n## Evidence Mapping Table\n| Claim/Event | Article IDs | Date Support | Confidence (0-1) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Kishane Thompson runs best time since Bolt | [art_general_20250628_018_f38232af] | 2025-06-28 | 1.0 | Fast, but not a WR |\n| Fred Kerley suspended | [art_general_20250813_028_f4db194f] | 2025-08-13 | 1.0 | Whereabouts failures |\n| Oblique Seville wins WAC 100m gold | [art_general_20250914_019_a383968e] | 2025-09-14 | 1.0 | Upstaged Lyles, no WR |\n\n## Uncertainties And Alternative Paths\nAn alternative path would have required absolute perfect conditions (maximum allowable tailwind, high altitude, peak tapering) aligning perfectly for a singular athlete like Lyles or Thompson. The uncertainty remains whether the current generation simply lacks an outlier talent on Bolt's level or if they haven't yet found the perfect race conditions to maximize their potential.\n",
